#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:3
msgid "Implementation notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:5
msgid ""
"The technology choices behind the engine, each with the alternative that "
"was rejected and the reason. These are decisions about *how the thing is "
"built*, useful to someone about to change it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:8
msgid ""
"They are deliberately kept out of the whitepaper, whose design section is"
" about the **algorithm** — pages, classification, segments, promises — "
"and not about which crates it is assembled from. See "
":doc:`../../../reference/design/index` for that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:13
msgid "The engine is Rust, and the parts that are not are named"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:15
msgid ""
"The engine was originally Python. It is now a Rust crate that Python "
"imports, and the whole of the logic — the data model, the pipelines, "
"loading a formats repository, promise resolution, writing the output — is"
" native."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:19
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** keeping the engine in Python and optimising the hot spots. "
"The hot spots were not localised; the work is per page and per block and "
"it is *all* of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:22
msgid ""
"**Two exceptions, and only two.** Python is reached in exactly two "
"places, and both are recorded as boundaries rather than left to grow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:25
msgid ""
"**Loading the PDF**, through PyMuPDF. There is no mature Rust equivalent "
"that reads a page's text with its layout, and writing one is not this "
"project. PyMuPDF is called once per document, and its output becomes a "
"native page immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:28
msgid ""
"**Running an author's pipes**, because an unstructured format *is* Python"
" by definition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:30
msgid ""
"The original page dictionary is kept next to the native page, so an "
"author's pipe receives what it expects while native pipes work on native "
"data — one conversion, not a round trip per block. Nothing else in the "
"crate mentions PyO3, and a Python error from an author's pipe is logged "
"with its traceback and converted to a typed error at the boundary; no "
"Python exception travels further."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:36
msgid "Oniguruma, not the ``regex`` crate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:38
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** the ``regex`` crate, which is faster and is what a Rust "
"programmer reaches for by reflex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:41
msgid ""
"Formats repositories are full of patterns already written by their "
"authors in Python/PCRE syntax, with backreferences and lookaround. The "
"``regex`` crate does not support them, by design. Choosing it would have "
"meant rewriting every existing pattern and telling every future author "
"that their regex knowledge does not apply here. The engine uses ``onig`` "
"so that a pattern that worked yesterday works today."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:48
msgid "The public API is a facade, not the module tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:50
msgid ""
"Everything reachable from ``api`` is a promise to library users; "
"everything else is an internal tree that may be reorganised at any time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:53
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** exporting the module tree directly, which is free and is "
"what most crates do. It makes every file move a breaking change, and it "
"publishes as commitments the fifty types that exist only because the code"
" is split into files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:57
msgid ""
"The facade is deliberately narrower than the tree, with one recurring "
"exception: a facade type drags in the types of its own public fields and "
"signatures. Exposing a block without its value type would hand callers a "
"struct they cannot read; exposing a fallible function without its error "
"type would hand them a ``Result`` they cannot match on. Those are not "
"surface creep, they are what makes a facade usable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:64
msgid "Typed errors everywhere, and no panics on a user path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:66
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** exceptions carried across the boundary, and ``unwrap`` on "
"values that came from a file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:69
msgid ""
"One error enum per module, built with ``thiserror``. Panics remain only "
"where the input genuinely cannot come from outside, and where they do, "
"the module says so. Two inherited panics are documented limits rather "
"than pending fixes — a deliberate choice to record a known edge instead "
"of pretending it was handled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:75
msgid "No pandas, no Pydantic, no Pandera"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:77
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** reading the formats repository's CSVs with pandas and "
"validating with Pandera, as the Python original did."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:80
msgid ""
"The joins involved are lookups on a derived key — a hash map, ten lines. "
"The validations are per-row checks that need to report **the row "
"number**, because these files are edited in a spreadsheet by people who "
"will never see a stack trace. Heavy dataframe dependencies bought a "
"concise expression of something that was not hard, and made the error "
"messages worse."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:85
msgid ""
"Dates are hand-written for the same reason: parse, format, compare, no "
"calendar arithmetic. A date library would have been a dependency serving "
"three functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:89
msgid "Serialisation is derived, so fixtures are readable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:91
msgid ""
"Everything crossing a segment derives serde's traits. The consequence "
"that matters is not internal: the per-page test fixtures are **JSON**, "
"produced and consumed by the same derivation, instead of Python pickles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:95
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** keeping pickle compatibility with the existing fixtures. It"
" would have frozen the data model to Python's object layout, and pickles "
"are opaque in a diff — a regression in a fixture should be something you "
"can *see*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:100
msgid "One logging system, three destinations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:102
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** one log stream with levels, which is the default answer "
"everywhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:104
msgid ""
"What a person watches while a run happens and what a tool parses "
"afterwards are different artefacts, and serving both from one stream "
"makes each worse. So: stderr for watching; a JSON-lines file for tools; "
"``.log.csv`` as the extraction's own audit trail, anchored to pages and "
"coordinates. See :doc:`../../user/logging`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:109
msgid ""
"Only two of the three are there on an ordinary run. The JSON-lines file "
"exists at ``trace`` and nowhere below it: it answers *everything the "
"engine did, in order, with the module and the error chain attached*, "
"which is a question only the maximum verbosity answers, and creating it "
"at every verbosity meant that even ``-q`` dropped a file into the "
"directory the run was started from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:114
msgid ""
"Which is also why neither log is created by ``tracing_setup::init``. "
"Whether there is to be a JSON-lines file, and where ``.log.csv`` goes, "
"are both answers that live in a configuration that cannot be resolved "
"until logging is already running; so ``init`` installs the subscriber and"
" creates nothing, the records accumulate, and "
"``LogHandle::settle_verbosity`` and ``set_csv_dir`` settle the two of "
"them the instant the configuration resolves. The level filters are "
"reloadable for the same reason — raising a verbosity after the fact means"
" rebuilding ``tracing``'s per-callsite interest cache, not just changing "
"a comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:122
msgid ""
"That file is where the structured error record lands — its ``Debug`` "
"form, its message and its whole ``source()`` chain, under the ``error`` "
"key of a line — because none of the three survives being flattened into a"
" sentence on stderr."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:126
msgid ""
"This whole area was tuned against a real complaint and measured. The "
"first instrumentation pass made a 1,140-page job take **19 minutes** and "
"produced a **2.8 GB** log file. After tuning: **13 seconds** and **609 "
"rows**, doing the same work. Instrumentation nobody can afford to leave "
"on is instrumentation that does not exist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:132
msgid "Processes and threads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:134
msgid ""
"The reasoning, the measurements and the two levels that the measurements "
"cancelled are part of the algorithm's design and live in "
":doc:`../../../reference/design/parallelism`. Two notes belong here "
"instead, because they are about the implementation rather than about the "
"design:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:138
msgid ""
"the page-level thread pool is **owned by the crate**, in a ``OnceLock``, "
"and is not rayon's global pool. A library must not seize the thread pool "
"of the program embedding it — and this library is also a Python extension"
" module, so the embedding program is a real thing with its own plans;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:142
msgid ""
"job-level parallelism is child **processes** of the same binary, driven "
"through a hidden ``--internal-worker`` flag and a two-file JSON protocol "
"in a temporary directory. The flag is hidden because it is not a user "
"interface but the channel between two copies of one program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:147
msgid "Documentation: one Sphinx site, rustdoc beside it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:149
msgid ""
"**Rejected:** mdbook for the prose, which reads better and is pleasanter "
"to write."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:151
msgid ""
"Three reasons survived scrutiny: the validation section already exists "
"and is live content that cannot be rewritten for free; publishing is "
"already configured; and enabling MyST means the prose is written in "
"Markdown *inside* Sphinx, which is most of what mdbook was wanted for, "
"without a second toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:156
msgid ""
"A fourth reason was offered and turned out to be **false** on measurement"
" — that four translations already existed. There is one partial "
"translation. The decision stands on the other three, and the false one is"
" recorded here because a reason that does not hold should stop being "
"repeated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:160
msgid ""
"rustdoc is generated and published as a sub-path, not transcribed into "
"``.rst``. A hand-written copy of the Rust API goes stale in a week."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:164
msgid "Some things were deliberately not redesigned"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:166
msgid ""
"The PDF line model, the line selections and the tabularizer were carried "
"over essentially as they were, and improvements noticed during the port "
"were **reported rather than applied**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/advanced/implementation-notes.rst:169
msgid ""
"This is a decision, not an omission. That code has been used for a long "
"time, its behaviour is what the existing formats depend on, and a rewrite"
" would have been risk without a user asking for it. The place to record "
"an improvement you cannot justify is a note, not a commit."
msgstr ""

